## What decided it
The era's named mechanic *was* the contract. The bank cap is both the ranking ceiling and an income switch. Generation 81 banked 198 of a default 200 here because its ladder stalled behind an unfillable sluice rung and never reached the cap-raisers. Retiring a rung that runs out of candidates — one `if` — got both stockpiles up and both to tier 2, lifting the cap to **680**.

The map itself has not moved: claim, all six anchors, the spring, the roster, the cost curves and the idle floor (143.300 s, wave 4) all reproduce generation 6's numbers to the decimal. Era 6 re-surveyed this map's rendering, not its rules.

## Two things worth flagging
- **`threats.alive` saturates here by design**, and I had it in an urgency predicate. It fired nearly every view, bypassed the bank gate, and bought 445 gold of surge fort after t=360 for a hero whose minimum was 86.3%.
- **The correction went the wrong way, and that is the more useful result.** Attempt-1 removed that clause and banked **89 instead of 328**: the leaner fort let works get wrecked, which re-armed urgency anyway and drained the purse in the window it should have been refilling. On a gold-ranked contract, over-buying defence early is how you afford to stop spending late.

